sections.content:
Voditelj postavlja provokativno pitanje: tko je „veći Hrvat" — čovjek koji je došao iz Nepala pa na utakmici uzeo hrvatsku zastavu, ili netko tko generacijama živi u Hrvatskoj, a na Dan državnosti ne istakne zastavu i ne ode glasati?

Gošća priznaje da je primjer ekstreman, ali ističe apsurd: lakše je odjenuti dres ili istaknuti zastavu nego otići na birališta. Na uobičajene izgovore — kiša, hladnoća, zimske čizme — odgovara kratko: „O čemu pričamo, izlazi vanka i glasaj."

Voditelj tada iznosi najčešći *feedback* koji dobiva u razgovorima s ljudima: **„pa nema dobrog izbora"**. Marijana Šarolić Robić na to nudi niz protupitanja i rješenja:

- ako nema dobrog izbora — **napravi dobar izbor**: kandidiraj se ti ili netko tvoj;
- jesi li uopće potrošio vrijeme i pročitao programe, ili si zaključivao na temelju *clickbaitova* koje plasiraju botovi „Bog te pita čiji";
- možda zaista postoji netko tko korelira s tvojim vrijednostima, a ti to nisi provjerio iz lijenosti;
- ako nikoga nema — budi nezavisni kandidat, budi na listi i zaokruži sam sebe.

„Pa upravo to, imat ću barem jednu listu", nadovezuje se voditelj, dok gošća zaključuje da je poanta cijele priče ohrabriti ljude da se aktiviraju umjesto da unaprijed kapituliraju.

sections.content_en:
The host asks a provocative question: who is the "greater Croat" — a man who came from Nepal and then took a Croatian flag at a match, or someone who has lived in Croatia for generations, and on Statehood Day does not display the flag and does not go to vote?

The guest admits that the example is extreme, but points out the absurdity: it is easier to put on a jersey or display a flag than to go to the polls. To the usual excuses — rain, cold, winter boots — she answers briefly: "What are we talking about, go outside and vote."

The host then presents the most frequent *feedback* he receives in conversations with people: **"why, there is no good choice"**. Marijana Šarolić Robić offers a series of counter-questions and solutions to that:

- if there is no good choice — **make a good choice**: run yourself or someone of yours;
- did you even spend time and read the programs, or did you make conclusions based on *clickbait* planted by bots "God only knows whose";
- maybe there really is someone who correlates with your values, and you did not check it out of laziness;
- if there is no one — be an independent candidate, be on the list, and circle yourself.

"Well, exactly that, I will have at least one list", the host follows up, while the guest concludes that the point of the whole story is to encourage people to get active instead of capitulating in advance.

sections.content:
Voditelj identitet hrvatskog domoljublja veže uz nekoliko stotina tisuća glasača koji HDZ-u ostaju vjerni još od devedesetih, kao svojevrsni *legacy*. Marijana Šarolić Robić dodaje da dio ljudi jednostavno ne zna gdje bi drugdje glasao — i tu izriče jednu od najoštrijih kritika u epizodi.

Ne voli, kaže, to „sve crno-bijelo, ili si Hrvat ili nisi Hrvat". Nije, poručuje, devedeset i prva: „Odradili smo to, ajmo se sad baviti time kako danas živjeti bolje." Nabraja što bi trebale biti stvarne teme:

- dostojanstvena mirovina za sve;
- pristup svemu što ljudima treba;
- najbolje moguće obrazovanje za djecu;
- najbolja moguća zdravstvena skrb u bolesti.

Umjesto toga, tvrdi, politika se bavi „bojanjem" — jesi li crveni ili zeleni. Sarkastično nabraja da čovjek može biti i crven i zelen, „bijeli, crni, na točkice ili na prugice ili 3D", sve dok radi ono što društvo od njega traži: plaća poreze kako treba, izvršava građanske dužnosti i izlazi na izbore. Jednostavnije je, kaže, brzo se podijeliti po bojama nego razgovarati o tome tko će zatrpati rupu. **„A rupa i dalje stoji. I svima upadaju kotači — i crvenima i zelenima"**, zaključuje, uz opasku da oni koji dođu s tenkovima naprave samo nove rupe.

Slijedi kratka digresija: voditelj primjećuje da se za vrijeme svjetskog nogometnog prvenstva praktički nije ratovalo i da su i mediji utihnuli, dodajući u šali da je i Donald Trump „stao sa svime samo da svjetsko prođe" te da FIFA sluša kako Amerika mora biti prvak.

sections.content_en:
The host links the identity of Croatian patriotism to several hundred thousand voters who have remained loyal to the HDZ since the nineties, as a kind of *legacy*. Marijana Šarolić Robić adds that part of the people simply do not know where else to vote — and here she voices one of the sharpest criticisms in the episode.

She does not like, she says, this "all black-and-white, either you are a Croat or you are not a Croat". It is not, she says, ninety-one: "We did that, let us now deal with how to live better today." She lists what the real topics should be:

- a dignified pension for all;
- access to everything that people need;
- the best possible education for children;
- the best possible healthcare in illness.

Instead, she claims, politics deals with "coloring" — whether you are red or green. She sarcastically lists that a person can be red, green, "white, black, spotted, or striped, or 3D", as long as he does what society demands of him: pays taxes properly, carries out civic duties, and turns out to vote. It is simpler, she says, to divide quickly by colors than to talk about who will fill in the hole. **"And the pothole still stands. And everyone's wheels fall in — both red and green"**, she concludes, with the remark that those who come with tanks make only new holes.

A brief digression follows: the host observes that during the World Cup there was practically no warfare and that the media also fell silent, adding jokingly that even Donald Trump "paused everything just for the World Cup to pass" and that FIFA listens to how America must be the champion.

sections.content:
Voditelj postavlja ključno strateško pitanje: kad bi Hrvatska bila prva ili među prvima, svaki novoosnovani start-up koji za sedam-osam godina postane jednorog imao bi sjedište ovdje — a s njim, dodaje gošća, i **porez na dobit**.

No Marijana Šarolić Robić inzistira da je financijski učinak tek jedan sloj. Puno važniji je, kaže, „soft layer" — percepcija zemlje koja potiče inovativnost, poduzetništvo i tehnološki razvoj. To je, uspoređuje, kao brend jedne firme: u bilancu možeš upisati koliko *goodwill* vrijedi, ali se zapravo ne može platiti. „To je zaista neprocjenjivo."

Zato zagovara aktivnu kampanju: da Hrvatska prva ispravno implementira regulativu i da poruči — dođite, osnujte hrvatsku varijantu EU Inc-a. Bilo bi divno, kaže, da naši političari o tome počnu razmišljati „umjesto da se bave ustašama, četnicima i ne znam čime", odnosno da se okrenu stvarima koje stvaraju vrijednost na razini društva. Zamišlja Španjolca ili Indijca koji dolazi ovamo, osniva kompaniju u Hrvatskoj, zapošljava ljude, plaća poreze i eventualno jednom napravi exit.

Slijedi objašnjenje logike koja pokreće cijelu tehnološku industriju:

- svi žele biti Silicijska dolina jer tamo postoji ideja da **snovi postaju stvarnost** — da ideja može postati proizvod i usluga;
- svaka firma želi biti *first to market*;
- želi plivati u **blue oceanu**, a ne u *red oceanu* u kojem se ratuje cijenama i količinama;
- želi biti toliko inovativna da konkurencije praktički nema — to je, kaže, „obećana zemlja svih kompanija";
- tehnološka inovacija je toliko ispred tržišta da si automatski **trend setter**, a ne netko tko emulira i zaostaje.

Hrvatska se, zaključuje, unutar jedinstvenog europskog tržišta može pozicionirati upravo kao takva destinacija: zemlja u koju ljudi svjesno dolaze zbog kvalitete života, sigurnosti i hrane „od polja do stola", a odakle mogu doslovno raditi i globalno prodavati svoj proizvod. Krajnji cilj nije samo zaustaviti odlazak Hrvata, nego postići i obrnuti efekt — dolazak stranaca.

sections.content_en:
The host asks a key strategic question: if Croatia were the first or among the first, every newly founded start-up that becomes a unicorn in seven to eight years would have its headquarters here — and with it, the guest adds, **corporate income tax**.

However, Marijana Šarolić Robić insists that the financial effect is only one layer. Much more important is, she says, the "soft layer" — the perception of a country that fosters innovation, entrepreneurship, and technological development. That is, she compares, like the brand of a company: on the balance sheet you can enter how much *goodwill* is worth, but it cannot actually be paid for. "That is truly priceless."

Therefore, she advocates an active campaign: for Croatia to be the first to properly implement the regulation and send the message — come, establish the Croatian variant of EU Inc. It would be wonderful, she says, for our politicians to start thinking about this "instead of dealing with Ustaše, Chetniks, and I don't know what", that is, to turn to things that create value at the level of society. She imagines a Spaniard or an Indian coming here, founding a company in Croatia, employing people, paying taxes, and eventually making an exit one day.

An explanation of the logic that drives the entire technology industry follows:

- everyone wants to be Silicon Valley because there the idea exists that **dreams become reality** — that an idea can become a product and service;
- every company wants to be *first to market*;
- it wants to swim in a **blue ocean**, not in a *red ocean* in which people wage war with prices and quantities;
- it wants to be so innovative that competition practically does not exist — that is, she says, "the promised land of all companies";
- technological innovation is so far ahead of the market that you are automatically a **trend setter**, not someone who emulates and lags behind.

Croatia, she concludes, within the single European market can position itself precisely as such a destination: a country where people consciously come because of the quality of life, safety, and food "from farm to table", and from where they can literally work and globally sell their product. The ultimate goal is not only to stop the departure of Croats, but also to achieve the reverse effect — the arrival of foreigners.

sections.content:
Marijana Šarolić Robić ne vjeruje da cijelo društvo može podijeliti istu viziju, ali vjeruje u **kritičnu masu ljudi** koja gura u istom smjeru i stvara platformu na kojoj se inovira, usuđuje i igra. Ta priča, naglašava, nije ograničena na Hrvatsku ni na europski gospodarski prostor, nego je globalna — jer isto to rade i Kinezi sa svojim vrijednostima, i Indijci, i Amerikanci.

To formulira kao pravilo koje svijet oduvijek slijedi: **„Ti svojom ekonomijom širiš i svoju kulturu."** Upravo zato smatra da europske vrijednosti treba čuvati, jer u sebi nose:

- blagostanje i dijeljenje bogatstva;
- razinu pristupa zdravlju i edukaciji kakvu druge zemlje nemaju;
- mogućnost da se studira praktički besplatno;
- pravo na besplatno zdravstvo;
- slobodu da radiš posao koji želiš ako si adekvatno educiran.

Puno država u svijetu, podsjeća, te slobode i ta prava nema, a mi ih uzimamo zdravo za gotovo iako to apsolutno nisu. Uz to dodaje i „Bogom danu lokaciju", uz napomenu da o geopolitici neće govoriti: društveno uređenje nije savršeno, ali je „puno, puno bolje nego na puno drugih mjesta".

Svoj pogled na EU Inc zaokružuje nadom: vjeruje da će inicijativa zaživjeti kako treba i da će do kraja godine biti izglasana u Europskom parlamentu. CroStartup će, obećava, sa svoje strane raditi sve da se Hrvatska prepozna — možda i kao brend — kao zemlja koju će start-upovi birati. Pritom odbacuje ideju međusobne konkurencije unutar Unije: **„Nema tu borbe između hrvatskih i njemačkih start-upova ili francuskih, to su gluposti, to su sve europski start-upovi."** Cilj je da ne odlaze u Delaware ili London zato što u Hrvatskoj, Njemačkoj ili Francuskoj ne mogu normalno poslovati.

sections.content_en:
Marijana Šarolić Robić does not believe that the whole society can share the same vision, but she believes in a **critical mass of people** that pushes in the same direction and creates a platform on which people innovate, dare, and play. That story, she emphasizes, is not limited to Croatia or to the European Economic Area, but is global — because the Chinese do the same with their values, and the Indians, and the Americans.

She formulates this as a rule that the world has always followed: **"Through your economy you also spread your culture."** That is precisely why she believes European values should be preserved, because they carry within themselves:

- prosperity and wealth sharing;
- a level of access to health and education that other countries do not have;
- the opportunity to study practically for free;
- the right to free healthcare;
- the freedom to do the job you want if you are adequately educated.

Many countries in the world, she recalls, do not have these freedoms and these rights, and we take them for granted although they absolutely are not. Along with this, she also adds a "God-given location", with the note that she will not speak about geopolitics: the social system is not perfect, but it is "much, much better than in many other places".

She rounds off her view on EU Inc with hope: she believes that the initiative will come to life properly and that by the end of the year it will be voted on in the European Parliament. CroStartup will, she promises, do everything on its part for Croatia to be recognized — perhaps also as a brand — as a country that start-ups will choose. In doing so, she rejects the idea of mutual competition within the Union: **"There is no battle between Croatian and German start-ups or French ones, those are nonsense, those are all European start-ups."** The goal is that they do not leave for Delaware or London because in Croatia, Germany, or France they cannot do business normally.
